SERIES = "English at Work"
TITLE = "The First Sales Call"
SUMMARY = "cold-call a stranger in English — earn twenty seconds, then earn two minutes, then leave well on a no"
INTRO = ("You sell something good. She has never heard of you, didn't ask you to call, and has a meeting in six minutes. "
         "Everything you'll ever win from this number is decided in your first two sentences.")

PANELS = [
"""        <h2>The Scene <span class="em">🎬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">The hardest conversation in professional English: the one the other person never asked for.</p>

        <p>You work for a firm that makes energy-monitoring kit for factories — genuinely good kit, the kind that pays for itself in a year. Your list says Ferreira, operations director, mid-sized plant, currently with a competitor. Your native-speaker colleagues make these calls on charm. You're going to make them on structure — and structure, it turns out, beats charm.</p>

        <p>The cold call has a physics all its own. She owes you nothing. Her default answer is no, her finger is on the red button, and every second of her attention is borrowed, not given. So the call is built as a ladder of small honest permissions: twenty seconds → two minutes → one page by email. Never the meeting, never the deal — not today. The seller who asks small and true gets further than the one who asks big and shiny.</p>

        <p>And the ending matters more than it seems. Most of these calls end in no. The craft is leaving each no so cleanly that the door stays warm — because the supplier she's "well looked after" by today will, one day, fail her. On that day, the rep who left well gets the call.</p>

        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>What makes you hang up on a cold caller in the first ten seconds? Be precise — what exactly do they do wrong?</li>
            <li>Selling in a second language: what's actually harder — the words, or the confidence to be brief?</li>
        </ol>

        <div class="whos">
            <div class="who"><b>You</b><span>Knows the product cold, knows why her plant specifically is on the list, and wants two minutes — not a wedding.</span></div>
            <div class="who"><b>Ms Ferreira</b><span>Operations director. Brisk, fair, allergic to scripts. Has a supplier, a meeting in six minutes, and — buried somewhere — a real problem.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="tip"><b>First:</b> read the conversation and watch the ladder: twenty seconds, honestly asked → relevance in one sentence → questions before pitch → the one-page close. Count how many times You asks for something small instead of something big.</div>""",

"""        <h2>The Conversation <span class="em">💬</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Tuesday, 9:50am — the golden window between the morning rush and the meetings. She picks up on the second ring.</p>

        <div class="dialogue">
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>Ferreira.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>Ms Ferreira — my name's Alex Costa, from Meridian Energy Systems. You don't know me, and this is a sales call. Have I caught you at a terrible time, or do you have two minutes?</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>…Points for honesty. You've got until my coffee's poured. Go.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>Then I'll be quick. The reason I'm calling you specifically is that your plant runs three shifts, and three-shift plants are where our monitoring pays back fastest — usually inside a year. Before I say anything else, though — is energy cost something you deal with at all, or does that sit elsewhere?</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>No, that's mine. It sits painfully on my desk, in fact. But we've got monitoring — we're with Enerlog, have been for years.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>Enerlog's solid kit. Can I ask what you're using it for at the moment — the full metering, or just the site-level dashboard?</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>…Site-level. The machine-level stuff was quoted, but the price was absurd.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>That's the gap we live in, as it happens. Machine-level monitoring, but priced per line rather than per point — compared to what that quote probably looked like, it usually works out at about a third.</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>A third. Mm. Everyone's cheaper on the phone, Mr Costa. And I'm not switching suppliers because of one call, you understand — the audit alone would eat any saving.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>Completely fair — and I'm not asking you to change anything today. Enerlog stays, the dashboard stays. Machine-level is an addition, not a replacement — it sits alongside what you have.</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>…Alongside. You didn't lead with that.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>I should have. So — how about I send one page: what it measures, what it costs per line, and the payback maths for a three-shift plant. If it's not relevant, you bin it and I won't chase you.</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>One page. Not a deck, Mr Costa. If a deck arrives, I delete it on principle.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>One page, no deck — you have my word. And if it does look interesting, when's a better week to try you? I know this one's bad.</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>After the twentieth. Budget review's done by then, which — as you clearly know, since you knew my shift pattern — is not a coincidence you invented.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>I'd love to claim that level of planning. After the twentieth, then. And if the page convinces you you're fine as you are — fair enough. It sounds like you're well looked after.</div>
            <div class="say say-a"><span class="who-label">Ms Ferreira</span>We are. Mostly. …Send the page. And Mr Costa — "this is a sales call" was the only reason you got past hello. Most of you pretend to be my old friend.</div>
            <div class="say say-b"><span class="who-label">You</span>Most of us should stop. If anything changes before the twentieth, you've got my number — thanks for hearing me out, Ms Ferreira.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="q">
            <p><b>From memory — don't scroll back up.</b></p>
            <ol class="bigq">
                <li>What two things does You say in the first ten seconds that most cold callers never say?</li>
                <li>What's the real gap You finds — and which question uncovered it?</li>
                <li>What exactly has been agreed by the end of the call? List every commitment, both sides.</li>
            </ol>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal answers</button>
            <div class="rev">
                <b>1.</b> "You don't know me, and this is a sales call" — the honest label; and "have I caught you at a terrible time, or do you have two minutes?" — asking permission instead of stealing time. She says herself it's the only reason the call survived hello.<br><br>
                <b>2.</b> She has site-level monitoring but not machine-level — the quote was "absurd". Uncovered by "Can I ask what you're using it for at the moment — the full metering, or just the site-level dashboard?" Questions found the gap; the pitch only had to fit it.<br><br>
                <b>3.</b> You sends exactly one page (no deck, no chasing); she reads or bins it; any follow-up waits until after the twentieth, when budget review is done. Nobody bought anything — and the call was a success.
            </div>
        </div>""",

"""        <h2>Useful Phrases <span class="em">🧰</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Ten phrases for calling strangers who didn't ask you to — sales, partnerships, recruitment, fundraising. Say each out loud BEFORE you reveal it.</p>

        <div class="deliver"><b>Say it like you mean it.</b> The cold caller's tell is speed — racing to fit the pitch in before the hang-up. Slow down to the speed of a person with something genuinely useful to say. And practise the first two sentences until they're automatic, because you will never say them calmly while you're still deciding them.</div>

        <h3>⏱️ The first twenty seconds</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 1</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> asking for time you haven't been given. Which opening?</p>
            <div class="choice">
                <div><b>A.</b> "Hi! How are you today? Lovely weather we're having!"</div>
                <div><b>B.</b> "Have I caught you at a terrible time, or do you have two minutes?"</div>
                <div><b>C.</b> "This will only take thirty seconds of your valuable time, I promise."</div>
            </div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the answer</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>B — "Have I caught you at a terrible time, or do you have two minutes?"</b><br>A is the fake-friend opening that gets calls killed on principle. C makes a promise everyone knows is false — no sales call has ever taken thirty seconds. B does something rare: it offers a genuine exit ("terrible time") before asking for anything, and it names an honest amount ("two minutes"). People given a real chance to say no often don't — and a yes you were given is worth ten you stole.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Have I caught you at a terrible time, or do you have two minutes?" Any unrequested call — including internal ones.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 2</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> proving this isn't a script — you chose her. Complete it:<br><br>"The reason I'm calling you ___ is that your plant runs three shifts."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"The reason I'm calling you specifically is that your plant runs three shifts."</b><br><b>Specifically</b> is the word that separates you from the list-readers — but only if what follows proves it. One researched fact about her operation ("three shifts") buys more trust than five minutes of product praise, because it answers the question every cold-called person is silently asking: <i>why me?</i> No real answer to that question, no real call.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "The reason I'm calling you specifically is ___." Do the homework the sentence demands, or don't dial.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 3</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> checking she's even the right person — before the pitch, not after. Complete it:<br><br>"Before I say anything else — is energy cost something you ___ ___ at all?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Before I say anything else — is energy cost something you deal with at all?"</b><br>The qualifying question, and the discipline is in "before I say anything else": you're refusing to pitch until you know the pitch has an owner. Wrong person? You've saved you both three minutes and can ask who's right. Right person? She's just told you the problem is hers — "it sits painfully on my desk" — and now she's participating, not receiving.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Before I say anything else — is ___ something you deal with at all?" The three-second filter that saves whole afternoons.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🔍 Questions before pitch</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 4</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> mapping what she already has — respectfully. Complete it:<br><br>"Can I ask what you're ___ at the ___?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Can I ask what you're using at the moment?"</b><br>The amateur hears "we're with Enerlog" and starts attacking Enerlog. The professional asks this — and listens. The status quo always has a gap (here: no machine-level metering, "the price was absurd"), and the gap is the only honest place a pitch can live. Note the manners of "can I ask" — you're requesting information about her house, and asking permission at the door.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Can I ask what you're using at the moment?" Sales, hiring, consulting — map before you pitch.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 5</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> pricing against what they know, not in the abstract. Complete it:<br><br>"Compared to what it's ___ you now, it usually works out at about a third."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Compared to what it's costing you now, it usually works out at about a third."</b><br>Numbers without anchors bounce off. "Nineteen thousand a year" means nothing down a phone line; "about a third of the quote that made you wince" lands instantly, because she supplies the other number herself. "Usually" keeps you honest — you haven't seen her plant yet, and pretending precision you don't have is how credibility dies.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Compared to what it's costing you now, it usually works out ___." Price is a comparison; always name the thing you're comparing to.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 6</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> collapsing the threat that's making her defensive. Put the pieces in order:</p>
            <div class="jumble"><span>to change anything</span><span>I'm not asking you</span><span>today.</span></div>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"I'm not asking you to change anything today."</b><br>The turning point of the whole call. Her defences were built against a rip-and-replace sale — the switch, the audit, the risk. This sentence dismantles the threat she was actually resisting, and what's left is a small, safe addition "alongside what you have". Watch for the moment the other person is saying no to something you're not selling; this is the sentence for it.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "I'm not asking you to change anything today." Lower the stakes and the wall comes down with them.</div>
        </div>

        <h3>🚪 Closing small, leaving warm</h3>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 7</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the ask — so small that refusing takes more effort than accepting. Complete it:<br><br>"How about I send one page — and if it's not relevant, you ___ it?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"How about I send one page — and if it's not relevant, you bin it?"</b><br><b>Bin it</b> — throw it away, no ceremony. The genius is in giving her the exit as part of the offer: one page, her verdict, no chasing. You're not asking for a meeting, a demo or a decision — you're asking for thirty seconds of reading she can abandon. Small honest asks convert; big shiny ones get "send me some information", which means goodbye.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "How about I send one page — and if it's not relevant, bin it?" The close that respects its way through the door. Then keep the promise: one page.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 8</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> turning "this is a bad week" into an appointment. Complete it:<br><br>"When's a better ___ to ___ you?"</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"When's a better week to try you?"</b><br>"Now's not a good time" is the softest brush-off in business — and most callers accept it as a no, which it isn't. This question converts it into a date, and it makes <i>her</i> the author of the follow-up: "after the twentieth" is her appointment now, not your intrusion. When you call on the twenty-first, you're keeping her arrangement.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "When's a better week to try you?" Every "not now" contains a "when" — go and get it.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 9</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> taking the no with grace — and meaning it. Complete it:<br><br>"Fair enough — it sounds like you're well ___ ___."</p>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal the phrase</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>"Fair enough — it sounds like you're well looked after."</b><br>The hardest sentence in sales, because it concedes the round completely — no "but", no last jab at the competitor, no door wedged open with your foot. And that's exactly why it works: praising her current arrangement marks you as someone who tells the truth even when it costs a sale. Suppliers stumble. Contracts end. The rep who said this is the one who gets remembered kindly when they do.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "Fair enough — it sounds like you're well looked after." Lose the round; win the relationship.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="q">
            <span class="move">Phrase 10</span>
            <p><b>The job:</b> the last line — the door left open without a foot in it. Say it out loud, then write it.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The call is ending on a no, or a maybe. Leave so they'd take your next call…"></textarea>
            <button class="rev-btn" onclick="toggleRev(this)">Reveal a strong version</button>
            <div class="rev"><b>e.g. "If anything changes, you've got my number — thanks for hearing me out."</b><br>Two gifts in one line. "If anything changes" plants the only seed worth planting — not <i>buy from me</i> but <i>remember me when the world moves</i>. And "thanks for hearing me out" acknowledges what she actually gave you: borrowed minutes she didn't owe. Gratitude for attention, not for money, is what separates a professional from a pest.<br><br><b>Take it anywhere:</b> "If anything changes, you've got my number." Then — the real skill — actually don't chase.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="keep">
            <h3>🔑 Make them yours</h3>
            <p>These ten work on any stranger's phone number you'll ever dial with a purpose. Say each answer out loud before you type it.</p>
            <p class="cue">"The reason I'm calling you specifically is ___." — pick a real prospect, client or contact. What's the researched fact that earns the word "specifically"?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The person, and the homework…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"Before I say anything else — is ___ something you deal with at all?" — build your qualifying question for what you actually sell or do.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your three-second filter…"></textarea>
            <p class="cue">"Fair enough — it sounds like you're well looked after." — think of a no you took badly. What would taking it this way have cost — and saved?</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The no, and the door it could have left open…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="english_at_work_phrase_bank.html">🧰 <span>Every phrase from every English at Work lesson, sorted by what it does.</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <div class="tip"><b>Quick test:</b> the ladder, out loud, against the clock — terrible time or two minutes → calling you specifically → do you deal with this at all → what are you using → costs about a third of that → not asking you to change anything → one page, bin it → better week → well looked after → you've got my number. Under ninety seconds, no rushing: dial-ready.</div>""",

"""        <h2>Your Turn <span class="em">🎭</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">Now the number is dialled and the twenty seconds are yours. Two rounds — caller, then the desk that answers.</p>

        <div class="timer">
            <div class="t-face" id="tface">02:00</div>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="startTimer(120)">▶ Two minutes</button>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="startTimer(180)">▶ Three minutes</button>
            <button class="t-btn" onclick="resetTimer()">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>

        <h3>Round 1 — you're calling</h3>
        <p>Sell something you actually know — your company's product, your own services, even an idea you'd pitch internally. Your teacher answers with one word and a raised eyebrow. You have their name and one researched fact.</p>

        <p class="cue">The first ten seconds: name, honest label, and the permission question.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="My name's… this is a sales call. Have I…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">Why them specifically — then qualify before you pitch a word.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The reason I'm calling you specifically… Before I say anything else…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">"We already have a supplier." Map what they're using, and find the gap.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Can I ask what you're using…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">They fear the switch. Collapse the threat, then close small.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="I'm not asking you to… How about I send one page…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">It ends in "not now" — or just no. Get the week, or take the no with grace, and leave the door warm.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="When's a better week… Fair enough… you've got my number."></textarea>

        <h3>Round 2 — now you're Ms Ferreira</h3>
        <p>Swap. Your teacher cold-calls you, and you're the operations director: six minutes to your meeting, a supplier you're mostly happy with, and a rule about decks. Give them exactly the resistance the world gives — no more, no less — and drop your one real problem only if they ask a question that deserves it.</p>

        <p class="cue">They open with fake friendship. Your response — and what would have worked instead?</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="How you answer, and what you're listening for…"></textarea>

        <p class="cue">They ask a genuinely good question. Reward it honestly — reveal the gap.</p>
        <textarea class="blank" placeholder="The problem you admit to…"></textarea>

        <div class="twist">
            <b>⚡ The twist</b>
            <p>Round 1 again — and mid-call, Ms Ferreira goes quiet, then says: "Actually. Our supplier failed their audit last month — I'm not supposed to say that. If your kit is real: two thousand points, installed by March, or don't waste my time. Can you do it?"</p>
            <p>The door you've been knocking on politely just swung wide open — and the honest answer is that March is impossible; May, realistically. She's offering you the deal and the deadline in one breath. Overpromise and you'll lose her forever in April. Tell the truth and you might lose her now. Sell it straight.</p>
            <textarea class="blank" placeholder="Your answer — the truth, and what you offer with it…"></textarea>
        </div>

        <div class="check">
            <b>Before you close the tab…</b>
            <ul>
                <li>Can you label the call honestly and still keep the line open?</li>
                <li>Can you prove "specifically" with one researched fact?</li>
                <li>Do you qualify and map before a word of pitch?</li>
                <li>Is your ask small enough to accept in one breath?</li>
                <li>Can you take a no so well they'd answer your next call?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>""",

"""        <h2>The Echo <span class="em">🕰️</span></h2>

        <div class="echo-card">
            <p>Around 1482, a thirty-year-old Leonardo da Vinci wanted a job at the court of Ludovico Sforza, ruler of Milan. He didn't send a portfolio of paintings. He sent what may be history's most famous cold pitch: a letter of ten numbered points, every one about <i>Ludovico's</i> problems — portable bridges for his campaigns, siege engines, cannon, armoured vehicles. The painting, the sculpture, the genius? One line, near the end, almost an afterthought.</p>
            <p>Leonardo had researched his prospect. Milan was arming for war; the Duke didn't need a portrait, he needed engineering. So the greatest artist alive led with the customer's need and buried his own brilliance — and it worked. He got Milan, and Milan eventually got <i>The Last Supper</i>, a mural that began, in a sense, as the upsell.</p>
            <p>Five centuries of selling have added nothing to the method: know why you're calling <i>this</i> person, speak to their problem before your product, and keep your masterpiece for the second meeting.</p>
        </div>

        <a class="morelink" href="short_history_leonardo_da_vinci.html">🎨 <span>Want the full story? The notebooks, the machines, and the man who pitched the Duke…</span><span class="go">Open →</span></a>

        <h3>Two bigger questions</h3>
        <ol class="bigq">
            <li>Leonardo led with bridges and buried the art. What do you lead with — what you're proudest of, or what they need? Should they ever be different things?</li>
            <li>Is there an honest version of selling? Describe the best salesperson who ever sold you something.</li>
        </ol>""",
]
